// T4 condition-2 probe: can anything tell an OWNED local from a BORROWED one?
//
// ADR 0004 proposes origin-emptiness as T4's ownership test. This file is the
// discriminating case, and it is deliberately IMPORT-FREE.
//
// Import-free matters. `.handoff.md` records that running local_escape on
// bench/daemon/daemon.goo printed a confident table whose every verdict was
// conservative for an UNRELATED reason: the imports had not resolved. The tell
// was `i`, a plain int loop counter, reading as escaping. One cause per verdict
// needs a reproduction with nothing to resolve.
//
// `borrowView` is the TrimPrefix shape that include/local_escape.h names as the
// hole: goostd/strings/strings.go returns `s[len(prefix):]`, a view into the
// argument's buffer. A release consumer acting on "does not outlive f" alone
// would free the CALLER's string.
package main

// Returns a VIEW into its argument. Owns nothing.
func borrowView(s string) string {
	return s[1:]
}

// Returns a FRESH allocation. The caller owns the result.
func makeOwned() *int {
	return new(int)
}

// Both locals die with f, so `local_escape` must report false for both.
// The question is whether ANYTHING distinguishes them, because T4 must
// release `a` and must NOT release `b`.
func f(s string) {
	a := makeOwned()
	b := borrowView(s)
	_ = a
	_ = b
}

// The direct-slice form, in case the call boundary is what loses the fact.
func g(s string) {
	c := s[1:]
	_ = c
}

// SCOPE, which is the other half of condition 2 and the one the handoff's T4
// plan did not account for.
//
// `outer` is bound ONCE and dies at h's exit. `inner` is rebound on EVERY
// iteration, so h leaks one object per iteration and holds only the last.
//
// local_escape reports ONE boolean per local NAME for the whole function, and
// the engine has no kill rule (escape_env_add_or_union only unions, escapes[]
// only ever becomes true). So a release emitted at FUNCTION EXIT frees whatever
// `inner` holds at that moment -- one of N -- and leaks the rest.
//
// This is the daemon's `err` shape exactly: `n, err := strconv.Atoi(f)` sits
// inside handle's `for` loop.
func h(n int) {
	outer := makeOwned()
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		inner := makeOwned()
		_ = inner
	}
	_ = outer
}
